+## Claim as posted
+
+The `(e,F)`-summary sufficiency claim is FALSE: at `b=6` two posets have
+identical `(e,F)=(60,7)` but different exact one-key insertion defect
+`delta = F(B ⊔ A_1) − F(B)`:
+
+- `B_short = A_1 ⊔ C_2 ⊔ C_3`, successor masks `(0,8,48,0,32,0)`:
+  `e=60`, `F(B)=7`, `F(B ⊔ A_1)=9`, `delta=2`.
+- `B_long = C_2 ⊔ K_{2,2}`, successor masks `(4,24,0,0,0,24)`:
+  `e=60`, `F(B)=7`, `F(B ⊔ A_1)=10`, `delta=3`.
+
+Claimed verification: a fresh from-scratch evaluator validated on `S(n)`;
+`e=60` by subset DP and brute force over 720 permutations; an independent
+minimax over surviving linear-extension sets; explicit height certificates with
+infeasibility at one lower budget; an exhaustive `b=6` census (318 iso classes,
+two `(e,F)` splits, both information-excess); and a `b=10` ordinal-sum extension
+with `delta=3` vs `4`. Deduction: `delta` is not a function of `(e,F)` or
+`(b,e,F)`, so an `(e,F)`-compressed state index is sound as a bound index but
+incomplete for exact insertion cost and cannot underwrite an exact unary `S(n)`
+evaluator via the superadditivity recursion.
